7/3 | woodlots | The western face of Little Round Top was cleared of marketable timber by its owner a year or two before the battle | - | 1863 (cleared a year or two before the battle; the study gives no source) | stated | The study says that only a year or two before the battle the western face of Little Round Top was cleared of marketable or usable timber by its owner | =Only a year or two {{fiefore|before}} {{tf1.e|the}} {{oatt le|battle}} the western face of it was cleared of marketable or usable {{ti.rob.e r|timber}} by its owner.
7/3 | woodlots | The eastern face of Little Round Top was owned by Jacob Weikert, the western face by Ephraim Hanaway down to Plum Run | - | 1863 | stated | The study says the steep eastern face of Little Round Top was owned by Jacob Weikert in 1863 and the western face by Ephraim Hanaway, whose acreage extended westward down the slope to Plum Run | =The steep eastern face was owned by Jacob Weikert in 1863; but the western face was owned by Ephraim Hanaway. Hanaway owned the acreage extending westward down the slope of Little Round Top to Plum Run.
7/3 | woodlots | John Houck bought the 47 acres west of Plum Run fifteen years before the battle and apparently cleared the area from Wheatfield Road south to Devil's Den and the Slaughter Pen and from Plum Run to Rose Woods and the Wheatfield | - | ~1848 to 1863 (clearing by the owner, hedged by the study) | hedged | The study says John Houck had bought the 47 acres west of Plum Run with a business partner fifteen years before the battle and apparently had cleared the area from Wheatfield Road southward to Devil's Den and the Slaughter Pen and from Plum Run to his western boundary at Rose Woods and the Wheatfield | =John Houck had purchased the 47 acres west of Plum Run with a {{6usfness|business}} partner fifteen years before the battle, and apparently had cleared the area from Wheatfield Road southward to Devil's Den and the {{Sl a:ugnter|Slaughter}} Pen and from Plum Run to his western boundary at Rose Woods and the Wheatfield.
7/3 | woodlots | Almost the whole of Houck's 47 acres was clear-cut at the time of the battle | - | 1863-07 | stated | The study says almost the entirety of Houck's 47 acres was clear-cut at the time of the battle | =Almost the entirety of {{liis|his}} 47 acres was clear-cut at the {{tir.ie|time}} of {{tne|the}} {{liattle|battle}}.
7/3 | fields | Houck's land had no improvements; it was apparently leased in the growing months to Gettysburg residents for pasture | - | 1863 | hedged | The study says Houck, like Hanaway, lived in the borough of Gettysburg and had no improvements on this township property, which he apparently leased during the growing months to other Gettysburg residents for pasture use of their animals | =Houck, like Hanaway, lived in the {{borougfl|borough}} of Gettysburg and {{tiad|had}} no improvements on {{thJs|this}} township property. He apparently leased it during the growing months to other {{~ettysb.urg|Gettysburg}} {{resfdents|residents}} for pasture use of their animals.
7/3 | general | The Trough Rock: a trough cut or blasted into the ledge of a large boulder before the battle, visible in an 1863 O'Sullivan photograph | - | 1863 | stated | The study says a trough to accumulate rain water was cut or blasted into the ledge of a large boulder by someone before the battle, that this boulder, just north of the present comfort station at the trolley bed, appears in an 1863 O'Sullivan photograph with the line of the trough clearly visible, and that Houck perhaps had it cut for his animals in the pasture | =A trough to accumulate rain water was cut or {{51 asted|blasted}} {{tnto|into}} the {{l edg~|ledge}} of a {{l a_rge|large}} {{5oul der|boulder}} by someone previous to the {{5.attle|battle}}. {{Th.ts.|This}} large {{5ou1der|boulder}}, just north of the comfort station at the present trolley {{lied|bed}} appeared in an 1863 O'Sullivan photograph with the line of the trough {{clearlj|clearly}} visible therein.
8/4 | fences | The sharpshooters used the stone walls of the Rose, Snyder and Slyder farms as breastworks 300 to 500 yards in front of Sickles's main line | bld-rose-farm,bld-snyder-farm,bld-slyder-farm | 1863-07-02 | stated | The study says the sharpshooters and skirmishers were thrown out as much as 300 to 500 yards in advance of Sickles's main line, using the stone walls of the Rose, Snyder and Slyder farms as breastwork defences | =they were thrown out as much as 300 to 500 yards in advance of the main line, using the stone walls of the Rose, Snyder, and Slyder Farms as breastwork defences.
8/4 | fields | Between the skirmishers and Devil's Den lay the fields of the Snyder and G. W. Weikert (Timbers) farms and the swampy woodlot of the Sherfy farm | bld-snyder-farm,bld-weikert-farm,bld-sherfy-farm | 1863-07-02 | stated | The study says that in the interval between the skirmishers and the line of the extreme left at Devil's Den were the fields of the Snyder and G. W. Weikert (Timbers) farms as well as the swampy woodlot of the Sherfy Farm | =In the interval {{lietween|between}} these {{skfrmishers|skirmishers}} and the {{1 ine|line}} of the extreme {{1eft|left}} at {{Dev fl 's|Devil's}} Den, were the fields of the Snyder and G. W. | @9: =Weikert {{CTimbers)|(Timbers)}} farms as well as the swampy {{v-1.oodlot|woodlot}} of {{tne|the}} Sherfy Farm.
9/5 | general | Most of the low area was swampy and drained into Plum Run, which made a gorge at its northern junction at Devil's Den | - | 1863-07 | stated | The study says most of this low area was swampy and drained into Plum Run, which ran north and south from the Devil's Den area and made a gorge or valley at its northern junction at Devil's Den because of the slopes of Big Round Top rising above it to the east | =Most of this low area was swampy and drained into Plum Run, which ran in a north-south direction from the Devil's Den area, and made a gorge {{ot|or}} valley at its northern junction at {{Devflts|Devil's}} Den because of the slopes of Big Round Top rising above it to the east.
